{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/*pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph.md .pose/workflows/review.md .pose/rules/security.md .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph.go; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph_test.go'","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-16\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on:          # prerequisites, inline list: other-spec, milestone:<roadmap>/<id>, roadmap:<slug>\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority:            # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority); ordering preference, not a blocker\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface:             # optional: minimal | standard | full (default) — minimal drops Tasks and derivable report sections; `pose specs facts <slug>` derives them\ndelivers: capability:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nFazer o `pose doctor` recomendar, e o `pose doctor --fix` escrever, o commit-graph do Git com filtros de Bloom de caminhos alterados num projeto cujo histórico é grande o bastante para pesar, e fazer o CI do pose-dist escrevê-lo antes dos gates.\n\n### Business value\nA spec `pose-review-ledger-is-read-once-per-operation` mediu, na primeira chamada `pose_closeout_state` de um servidor MCP no harne8, 1.116 comandos `git log -1 --format=%H -- <path>` somando 81 s: cada um percorre o histórico do pose-dist (3.528 commits) até achar o último commit que tocou o arquivo. Com o commit-graph com caminhos alterados (`git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths`), cada consulta cai de 47 ms para 2 ms, sem mudar a saída do Git, e a primeira chamada cai de 136 s para 59 s. É manutenção do repositório, que o motor não faz sozinho numa leitura de governança; o `doctor` é o lugar onde o POSE já recomenda e aplica remediações seguras.\n\n### Constraints\n- A verificação só lê arquivos do Git; não roda nada que escreva.\n- A correção só escreve sob o diretório de objetos do Git, é idempotente e reversível (apagar o arquivo devolve o estado anterior), como as demais do registro do `doctor --fix`.\n- Uma instalação nova, com histórico pequeno, continua sem passos pendentes.\n\n### Non-goals\n- Escrever o commit-graph durante uma operação de governança.\n- Manter o commit-graph atualizado a cada commit (o `git gc` e o `git maintenance` já o reescrevem preservando os filtros).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: When the project root is a Git repository whose HEAD has at least 500 commits, and Git's own trace of a last-commit lookup by path shows no changed-path Bloom filter consulted, `pose doctor` shall report the check `git.commit-graph` at level `next`, with the command that writes it; the judgement follows whichever commit-graph Git loads (its own object directory or an alternate, a single file or a chain).\n- R2: `pose doctor --fix --yes` shall remediate `git.commit-graph` by running `git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths` in the project root, after which the check shall report `ok`.\n- R3: A repository with fewer than 500 commits on HEAD, or one whose last-commit lookup Git answers from the filters, shall report `ok`; a project root that is not a Git repository, or has no commit on HEAD, shall not report the check; any other failure to read the state shall report `warn` with a bounded description of the failure.\n- R4: The pose-dist CI jobs that check out the full history shall write the commit-graph with changed paths before their gates.\n\n### Non-functional\n- A verificação roda três comandos Git de leitura e uma busca do último commit de um arquivo com o trace2 ligado.\n\n### Security\n- A correção roda o Git com argumentos fixos no root do projeto; nenhuma entrada do usuário entra no comando.\n\n### Compatibility\n- Saída do `doctor` ganha um check novo; o formato do relatório não muda.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n- `cli/doctor.go`: o check `git.commit-graph` e a entrada no registro de correções.\n- `cli/doctor_commit_graph.go`: a contagem do histórico e a pergunta ao Git, pelo trace2, se ele usa os filtros.\n- `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: o passo que escreve o commit-graph.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_commit_graph_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go\n- modified: .github/workflows/ci.yml\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\nComposto em produção pelo `pose doctor` e pelo `pose doctor --fix`. Check permanente `doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph-integration`, evidenceClass `integration`, required: `go test ./internal/cli -run 'TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph|TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory' -count=1`.\n\n### API/contract changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Data/storage changes\n- Nenhuma no `.pose/`; a correção escreve o commit-graph sob `.git/objects/info/`.\n\n### Technical risks\n- O evento `bloom` do trace2 é saída de diagnóstico do Git; uma versão que o renomeie faria o check recomendar a correção sempre. A correção é inofensiva, e o teste de integração com o Git real reprova se o evento sumir.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Check e correção `git.commit-graph` no `doctor` (R1, R2, R3).\n- [x] Passo do CI (R4).\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Testes com repositórios reais acima e abaixo do limite, com e sem filtros, com prova de reprovação.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Date: 2026-11-16\n- Context: o follow-up F1 da spec `pose-review-ledger-is-read-once-per-operation`; o maintainer pediu para escrever o commit-graph nos checkouts dele e seguir com o F1.\n- Options considered: (a) o motor escreve o commit-graph numa leitura de governança; (b) o `doctor` recomenda e o `doctor --fix` escreve; (c) só documentar.\n- Decision: (b), com o limite de 500 commits.\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n- Rationale: (a) faria uma leitura escrever no repositório, o que a read view trata como mudança de entrada; (c) não chega a quem não lê o manual; (b) usa o caminho de remediação segura que o POSE já tem. Abaixo de cerca de 500 commits a busca de histórico custa poucos milissegundos.\n- Consequences: um projeto grande vê um passo pendente até rodar a correção.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nTestes do `doctor` com repositórios Git reais; medição já registrada na spec de origem.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/cli -count=1`\n- Scope: pose-mcp\n- Expected: verde\n\n### Execution log\n- Date: 2026-11-16\n- Environment: Linux, Go 1.26, Git 2.56.\n- Notes: o teste monta uma instalação real, cria histórico acima de um limite reduzido e confere o check sem commit-graph (`next`, corrigível), depois da correção (`ok`), com um commit-graph sem filtros (`next`) e com um commit-graph dividido em camadas com filtros (`ok`); abaixo do limite, `ok`. Reprova quando a leitura ignora os chunks BIDX/BDAT, quando ignora a cadeia de camadas e quando a correção não está registrada. O guarda `TestEngineStartsGitOnlyThroughTheRawReader` reprovou a primeira versão, que chamava o Git direto; ela passou a usar `RawHistoryGit`. No checkout do pose-dist do maintainer, com o commit-graph escrito, o check dá `ok`. O `pose doctor` no harne8 passa de 900 s com o binário instalado antes desta spec, e por isso não foi medido lá.\n\n### Results summary\n- Successes: o `doctor` recomenda e escreve o commit-graph; o CI o escreve antes dos gates.\n- Failures: nenhuma.\n- Warnings: o `pose doctor` no harne8 leva mais de 900 s, já antes desta spec (follow-up F1).\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph check:doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph test:TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory\n- R4 [satisfied] O passo `Write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters` nos dois jobs de `.github/workflows/ci.yml` que fazem checkout do histórico inteiro.\n\n### Known gaps\n- Nenhuma.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n\nNão concluída.\n\n### Residual risks\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n- [open] F1: medir e reduzir o `pose doctor` no harne8, que passa de 900 s em Go, sem processo Git filho, já com o binário anterior a esta spec. (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:medium review:2026-11-23)\n\n### Review\n\nRodada 1 (run rrn-237e7d9be5f0c4f5, changes-requested): 4 bloqueadores, corrigidos. F2 a leitura ignorava diretórios de objetos alternativos; F3 preferia a cadeia de camadas ao arquivo único, ao contrário do Git; F4 aceitava cabeçalhos de versão não suportada e arquivos truncados. Os três vinham de reimplementar a leitura do commit-graph; o `doctor` deixou de ler os arquivos e passou a perguntar ao próprio Git: roda `git log -1 -- <path>` com `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT` e procura o evento `bloom`/`statistics` com algum filtro consultado, de modo que vale o grafo que o Git carrega, com as regras dele. O teste cobre um grafo só no alternate (`clone --shared`) e o arquivo único sem filtros ao lado de uma cadeia com filtros (`next`, porque o Git carrega o arquivo único). F5 qualquer erro de leitura suprimia o check; agora só a falta de repositório ou de commit em HEAD o suprime, e as demais falhas viram `warn` com a descrição limitada (`TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory`, com um commit pai apagado).\n\n# Workflow: Review\n\n## Objective\n\nVerify that a change is correct, production-safe, and aligned with its approved scope and specs.\n\n## Preconditions\n\n- Make the final diff readable through cohesive commits or batches.\n- Provide requirement and spec context.\n- Attach implementer validation evidence, including `pose validate` output.\n- Define acceptance criteria and expected risk.\n\n## Execution checklist\n\n1. Resolve `pose review bundle <scope> --explain` (or `pose_review_bundle`) when bundle policy is enabled; otherwise resolve `pose review-plan <scope> --explain`.\n2. Confirm the semantic scope, patch/tree identity, mapped components and bundle plan digest.\n3. Run required active tools first, evaluate recommended tools explicitly and leave completion-only tools deferred until an attestation exists.\n4. Select the plan's applicable rules explicitly and record them in the review.\n5. Resolve rule conflicts with the most restrictive option, prioritizing security for exposure, authorization, and integrity risks.\n6. Consult `.pose/knowledge/` for relevant handoffs, accepted risks, follow-ups, and decision logs.\n7. Check compliance with specs, contracts, and local instructions.\n8. Review functional correctness and edge cases.\n9. Evaluate security, observability, and performance risks.\n10. Require validation evidence from `.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json` proportional to risk.\n11. Identify regression and rollout or rollback risks.\n12. Run plan-selected assessments, including tech-debt and integration when applicable.\n13. Classify findings by severity and propose objective actions.\n14. **Produce a handoff** in `.pose/knowledge/` when findings result in accepted risk, post-merge monitoring, or deferred work (`pose new-knowledge handoff <slug>`); link it from the review.\n15. Seal the validated subject with `pose review bundle <scope> --seal`; stop if any input is unclassified or changed since preparation.\n16. Issue a final decision: approved, approved with reservations, changes requested, or rejected.\n17. Record the decision with `pose review attest <bundle-id> ... --plan-digest <sha256> --apply`; signed external envelopes use `pose review attest --envelope <path> --apply`.\n18. Run `pose review verify <scope>` and `pose review-check <scope>`; reject stale, incomplete, conflicting or transitively invalid reused criteria.\n19. Apply closeout only after verification reports `ready-to-close`; lifecycle, state and report refreshes must not change the sealed bundle.\n20. When Contributor Mode is active and review identifies linter false-positives, diagnostic frictions, or POSE rule gaps, stage an improvement proposal via `pose contribute stage --type enhancement`.\n\n## Required rule selection\n\nAttach this section to the review:\n\n```md\n## Rules applied during review\n- Change type: <feature|bugfix|refactor|documentation-update|mixed>\n- Workflow consulted: `.pose/workflows/<file>.md`\n- Rules selected:\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/security.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`, when installed)\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`\n  - [ ] `.pose/rules/knowledge-governance.md` when knowledge or process changes\n- Rationale for each selected rule: <one line per item>\n- Rules not applicable: <list and explain>\n```\n\n## Recurrence escalation\n\n- Trigger `.pose/workflows/recurrence-escalation.md` when recurring rework is uncovered and the threshold is met.\n- Require `recurrence_rework_uncovered` evidence for the 30-day period.\n- Require explicit links from the specialized workflow to applicable domain rules.\n- Require a formal keep, adjust, or discard decision after the 45-day pilot.\n\n## Minimum domain coverage\n\n- React UI: apply `frontend-react`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Go services: apply `backend-go`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Cluster deployment or infrastructure: apply `kubernetes`, `security`, and `documentation-style`.\n- Process, spec, workflow, rule, or report: apply `documentation-style`, `knowledge-governance`, and security when sensitive data is involved.\n- Cross-stack changes: apply every touched domain rule cumulatively.\n\n## Domain review checklist\n\n### Security\n\n- Confirm authentication, authorization, and least privilege when applicable.\n- Verify that code, configuration, manifests, docs, and logs contain no secrets.\n- Require applicable vulnerability and secret-scanning evidence.\n\n### Contracts\n\n- Confirm compatibility of public HTTP, event, schema, CLI, and file contracts.\n- Validate forward and backward compatibility for rollout and rollback.\n- Require a spec update for contract changes.\n\n### Observability\n\n- Verify structured logs and metrics without sensitive data.\n- Confirm that probes, health checks, and alerts reflect real behavior.\n- Preserve enough traceability for post-deployment diagnosis.\n\n### Validation\n\n- Require lint, typecheck, test, and build coverage proportional to risk.\n- Require executed `pose validate` evidence and relevant results.\n- Record environment limitations and residual validation risks.\n\n## Editorial checklist\n\n- Use imperative, actionable instructions.\n- Keep bullets short and avoid duplicated sections.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- Reference explicit files and paths.\n\n## Required outputs\n\n- A review decision with rationale.\n- The inspected effective plan, its component provenance and tool dispositions.\n- An immutable sealed review bundle and separate attestation bound to its exact digest.\n- A completed rule-selection section.\n- Findings with severity, evidence, and recommendation.\n- Recurrence classification by domain and cause with preventive actions.\n- An explicit statement about public contracts and compatibility.\n- Residual risks and safe-deployment conditions.\n- References to executed checks and collected evidence.\n\n## Complete multi-rule review example\n\n```md\n## Review Summary\n- Decision: approved with reservations\n- Change type: feature (Go API, React UI, and Helm)\n- Workflow: `.pose/workflows/feature.md`\n\n## Rules applied during review\n- `.pose/rules/backend-go.md` (extension `pose-rule-backend-go`): verified handlers, context, and error handling.\n- `.pose/rules/frontend-react.md` (extension `pose-rule-frontend-react`): verified accessibility and explicit loading and error states.\n- `.pose/rules/kubernetes.md` (extension `pose-rule-kubernetes`): verified resources, probes, and immutable images.\n- `.pose/rules/security.md`: verified authorization and absence of secrets.\n- `.pose/rules/documentation-style.md`: verified editorial consistency.\n\n## Checks and evidence\n- `pose validate`: passed\n- `go test ./...`: passed in the backend module\n- `pnpm lint && pnpm test`: passed in the frontend module\n- `helm template` and `kubectl apply --dry-run=client`: passed\n\n## Contracts and compatibility\n- Preserved `POST /v1/storage`.\n- Added optional `retentionDays`, preserving backward compatibility.\n\n## Findings\n- Medium: missing queue-saturation alert; add a metric and alert before production.\n- Low: frontend error lacks request-id context; improve observable UX.\n\n## Residual risks\n- Review did not simulate real cluster load.\n- Increase monitoring during the first 24 hours.\n```\n\n## CI failure interpretation\n\n- Treat `POSE check (strict)` as merge-blocking on protected branches.\n- Treat failed required checks in `POSE validate (strict)` as merge-blocking.\n- Attach pipeline artifacts: `pose-check.log`, `pose-validate.latest.log`, and the POSE report.\n- Classify optional-check failures as quality reservations with an owned remediation plan.\n- Reject changes that violate specs, public contracts, security, or safe rollout.\n\n## Phased rollout for unready modules\n\n- Enforce the current required checks immediately.\n- Use `moduleOverrides` to phase adoption without weakening global structural and required gates.\n- Promote optional checks to required by module with an owner and agreed window.\n\n### Optional-to-required promotion protocol\n\n- Select a pilot domain and map candidate checks with owners and explicit risks.\n- Measure success for four weeks and require a baseline of at least 95 percent.\n- Promote only eligible domains through `moduleOverrides`.\n- Update the validation matrix and quality policy in the same change set.\n- Monitor regressions and adjust rollout without removing established required gates.\n- Update specs and rules when rollout changes merge acceptance criteria.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- Resolve or formally accept every critical and high finding.\n- Make the final decision clear and actionable.\n- Support quality and security conclusions with evidence.\n- Preserve approved scope without unjustified drift.\n\n## Reviewer mode\n\n**Objective:** evaluate technical quality and production readiness with emphasis on correctness, risk, and scope.\n\n- **Focus:** functional correctness, spec consistency, regression and security risk, operability, validation quality, and actionable feedback.\n- **Anti-patterns:** approve without sufficient evidence; review style while ignoring functional risk; request unrelated changes; block progress on subjective preference.\n- **Handoff:** state the decision, severity and evidence for each finding, safe merge or deployment conditions, accepted risks, and recommended monitoring.\n# Rule: Security\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for authentication, authorization, sensitive data, external integrations, dependencies, and attack surfaces.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Apply least privilege to resource and credential access.\n- Store secrets only in appropriate secret-management mechanisms, never in code.\n- Validate and sanitize external input for its execution context.\n- Keep personal and confidential data out of plaintext logs and metrics.\n- Evaluate new dependencies for maintenance, licensing, and known vulnerabilities.\n- Test both positive and negative authentication and authorization cases.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Committing credentials, tokens, keys, or secrets in any versioned artifact.\n- Disabling TLS or certificate verification without a formally documented mitigation.\n- Trusting client input for authorization decisions.\n- Executing dynamic commands without adequate validation and escaping.\n- Ignoring critical security alerts without an approved exception record.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run secret scanning on the changed diff or scope.\n- Run the module's applicable dependency vulnerability scanner.\n- Pass the relevant authentication and authorization tests.\n- Complete a review for sensitive-data exposure in logs and configuration.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Delivery Evidence\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when writing or reviewing any document that **claims delivery,\ncompletion, or readiness**: status reports, handoffs, summaries, module READMEs,\ncurrent-state sections in specs or proposals, and messages that say something is complete.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Claim delivery only with attached, verifiable gate evidence: command and output\n  (`pose validate`, `go test`, `tsc`, `vitest`) or a link to the corresponding POSE report.\n- Use POSE lifecycle vocabulary: `draft`, `in-progress`, `done`, `blocked`,\n  `superseded`, or `abandoned`. Do not invent labels such as `completed` or `100% COMPLETE`.\n- Separate **implemented and verified** work from **planned or documented** work.\n  A plan describes intent; do not present that intent as current reality.\n- Reference the report or evidence that crossed the spec exit gate before using `done`.\n- Convert relative dates to absolute dates and stamp the verification date.\n- Declare exact source-tree artifact actions in the spec and reconcile them\n  against an immutable Git change set before claiming delivery.\n- Treat artifact declarations, Git observations and delivery verification as\n  separate evidence; none proves the others by itself.\n- For typed delivery targets, require a path from reconciled artifacts through\n  a production entrypoint to current structured validation results. A surface\n  requires reachability plus integration/e2e evidence; a composed capability\n  requires integration evidence.\n- Evidence answers for the component it ran in or for a component inside it: a\n  module-wide run covers its subtree, but a result from a directory inside a\n  component does not answer for the whole component. Coverage that rests only\n  on a containing module is reported as `inferred-coverage` — name it as such.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Claiming `100% COMPLETE` or production readiness without a green\n  `pose validate --strict` for every affected module.\n- Publishing contradictory delivery documents for the same scope; reconcile them first.\n- Merging code with completion documentation before the applicable POSE checks pass.\n- Mixing aspiration and verified state in one paragraph without clear labels.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `pose check --strict` for structure and spec status enums.\n- Run `pose validate --strict` for every module the document claims to deliver.\n- Run `pose lint-spec` when the document is a spec.\n- Run `pose artifact-check --strict` when the scope changes governed source-tree paths.\n- Run `pose surface-check --strict` for specs with `delivers` and `pose\n  roadmap-check --strict` before a roadmap claims completion.\n- `pose assess discover --if-stale --update-state` when closing specs to update completeness metrics, technical debt, and reports under `.pose/assessments/` and `.pose/state/`.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence over progress narratives when rules conflict.\n- When pressured to claim completion without a gate, record the real state\n  (`in-progress` or `blocked`) and the remaining exit conditions.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"bufio\"\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"strconv\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n)\n\n// commitGraphHistoryThreshold is the history size from which a last-commit\n// lookup by path is worth accelerating: below it a history walk costs a few\n// milliseconds (spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph).\nvar commitGraphHistoryThreshold = 500\n\n// errCommitGraphNotApplicable marks a root the check does not apply to: not\n// a Git repository, or a repository without a commit on HEAD.\nvar errCommitGraphNotApplicable = errors.New(\"not a Git repository with a commit on HEAD\")\n\n// commitGraphState is what doctor knows about a repository's commit-graph.\ntype commitGraphState struct {\n\tcommits int\n\t// filtersUsed is Git's own answer: a last-commit lookup by path read\n\t// changed-path Bloom filters from the commit-graph it loaded.\n\tfiltersUsed bool\n}\n\n// readCommitGraphState counts HEAD's history and asks Git whether a\n// last-commit lookup by path uses changed-path Bloom filters. Git reports the\n// filters it consulted in its trace2 \"bloom\" statistics, so whichever graph it\n// loads (the object directory's or an alternate's, a single file or a chain,\n// of any version it accepts) is judged as Git itself uses it. It only reads.\nfunc readCommitGraphState(root string) (commitGraphState, error) {\n\tif _, err := commitGraphGit(root, nil, \"rev-parse\", \"--verify\", \"--quiet\", \"HEAD^{commit}\"); err != nil {\n\t\treturn commitGraphState{}, errCommitGraphNotApplicable\n\t}\n\tcount, err := commitGraphGit(root, nil, \"rev-list\", \"--count\", \"HEAD\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn commitGraphState{}, fmt.Errorf(\"history of HEAD could not be counted: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tcommits, err := strconv.Atoi(strings.TrimSpace(count))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn commitGraphState{}, fmt.Errorf(\"history of HEAD could not be counted: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tstate := commitGraphState{commits: commits}\n\tif commits < commitGraphHistoryThreshold {\n\t\treturn state, nil\n\t}\n\tlisting, err := commitGraphGit(root, nil, \"ls-tree\", \"-r\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", \"HEAD\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn state, fmt.Errorf(\"files of HEAD could not be listed: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tpath, _, _ := strings.Cut(listing, \"\\x00\")\n\tif path == \"\" {\n\t\treturn state, nil\n\t}\n\ttrace, err := os.CreateTemp(\"\", \"pose-doctor-trace2-*.json\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn state, fmt.Errorf(\"Git trace could not be created: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\ttracePath := trace.Name()\n\ttrace.Close()\n\tdefer os.Remove(tracePath)\n\tif _, err := commitGraphGit(root, []string{\"GIT_TRACE2_EVENT=\" + tracePath}, \"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%H\", \"--\", path); err != nil {\n\t\treturn state, fmt.Errorf(\"last commit of a file could not be read: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(tracePath)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn state, fmt.Errorf(\"Git trace could not be read: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tstate.filtersUsed = traceUsedBloomFilters(raw)\n\treturn state, nil\n}\n\n// traceUsedBloomFilters reports whether a trace2 event stream carries a\n// \"bloom\" statistics event in which some filter answered a lookup.\nfunc traceUsedBloomFilters(raw []byte) bool {\n\tscanner := bufio.NewScanner(bytes.NewReader(raw))\n\tscanner.Buffer(make([]byte, 0, 64<<10), 1<<20)\n\tfor scanner.Scan() {\n\t\tvar event struct {\n\t\t\tCategory string `json:\"category\"`\n\t\t\tKey      string `json:\"key\"`\n\t\t\tValue    struct {\n\t\t\t\tMaybe         int `json:\"maybe\"`\n\t\t\t\tDefinitelyNot int `json:\"definitely_not\"`\n\t\t\t} `json:\"value\"`\n\t\t}\n\t\tif json.Unmarshal(scanner.Bytes(), &event) != nil {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif event.Category == \"bloom\" && event.Key == \"statistics\" && event.Value.Maybe+event.Value.DefinitelyNot > 0 {\n\t\t\treturn true\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn false\n}\n\n// writeCommitGraph writes the repository's commit-graph with changed-path\n// Bloom filters; it only writes under the Git object directory.\nfunc writeCommitGraph(root string) error {\n\t_, err := commitGraphGit(root, nil, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\")\n\treturn err\n}\n\n// commitGraphGit runs Git in root with extra environment and returns its\n// output; a failure carries Git's stderr, bounded.\nfunc commitGraphGit(root string, env []string, args ...string) (string, error) {\n\tcmd := posemodel.RawHistoryGit(root, args...)\n\tcmd.Env = append(cmd.Env, env...)\n\tvar stderr bytes.Buffer\n\tcmd.Stderr = &stderr\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tdetail := strings.TrimSpace(stderr.String())\n\t\tif len(detail) > 200 {\n\t\t\tdetail = detail[:200] + \"…\"\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn string(out), fmt.Errorf(\"git %s: %v %s\", args[0], err, detail)\n\t}\n\treturn string(out), nil\n}\npackage cli\n\nimport (\n\t\"bytes\"\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nfunc doctorCommitGraphFinding(t *testing.T, repo string) doctorFinding {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tvar finding doctorFinding\n\tinDir(t, repo, func() {\n\t\tvar out, errB bytes.Buffer\n\t\tMain([]string{\"doctor\", \"--json\"}, &out, &errB)\n\t\tvar report doctorJSON\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(out.Bytes(), &report); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"invalid JSON: %v\\n%s\", err, out.String())\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, f := range report.Findings {\n\t\t\tif f.Check == \"git.commit-graph\" {\n\t\t\t\tfinding = f\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\tif finding.Check == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"doctor reported no git.commit-graph check\")\n\t}\n\treturn finding\n}\n\nfunc gitInRepo(t *testing.T, repo string, args ...string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tif out, err := exec.Command(\"git\", append([]string{\"-C\", repo}, args...)...).CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"git %v: %v %s\", args, err, out)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc removeCommitGraph(t *testing.T, repo string) {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tinfo := filepath.Join(repo, \".git\", \"objects\", \"info\")\n\tfor _, name := range []string{\"commit-graph\", \"commit-graphs\"} {\n\t\tif err := os.RemoveAll(filepath.Join(info, name)); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\n// Spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph: R1 a long history Git walks\n// whole is a next step with a fix; R2 the fix writes the filters and the\n// check turns ok; R3 a short history needs none. The answer is Git's own, so\n// it follows whichever graph Git loads.\nfunc TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph(t *testing.T) {\n\trepo := doctorFixture(t)\n\toriginal := commitGraphHistoryThreshold\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { commitGraphHistoryThreshold = original })\n\tfor i := 0; i < 4; i++ {\n\t\t// Each commit changes a file of its own, so a lookup of another path\n\t\t// has commits the filters can rule out.\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(repo, \"zz-history.txt\"), []byte(strings.Repeat(\"x\", i+1)), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"add\", \"zz-history.txt\")\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"-m\", \"history\")\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 1 << 30\n\tif f := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); f.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a short history is not ok: %+v\", f)\n\t}\n\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 3\n\tmissing := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo)\n\tif missing.Level != \"next\" || missing.FixCode != \"git.commit-graph\" || missing.RemediationClass != remediationFixable {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a long history without a commit-graph: %+v\", missing)\n\t}\n\n\tinDir(t, repo, func() {\n\t\tvar out, errB bytes.Buffer\n\t\tMain([]string{\"doctor\", \"--fix\", \"--yes\", \"--only\", \"git.commit-graph\"}, &out, &errB)\n\t})\n\tif fixed := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); fixed.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the fix did not satisfy the check: %+v\", fixed)\n\t}\n\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--no-changed-paths\")\n\tif plain := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); plain.Level != \"next\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a commit-graph without changed-path filters is not recommended again: %+v\", plain)\n\t}\n\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\", \"--split\")\n\tif split := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); split.Level != \"ok\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a split commit-graph with changed paths is not recognized: %+v\", split)\n\t}\n\n\t// Review F3: with a single graph and a chain, Git loads the single graph.\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--no-changed-paths\")\n\tif both := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); both.Level != \"next\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unfiltered single graph beside a filtered chain is judged by the chain: %+v\", both)\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, repo)\n\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"commit-graph\", \"write\", \"--reachable\", \"--changed-paths\")\n\n\t// Review F2: a repository whose graph lives only in an alternate.\n\tshared := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"shared\")\n\tif out, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"clone\", \"-q\", \"--shared\", repo, shared).CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"clone --shared: %v %s\", err, out)\n\t}\n\tremoveCommitGraph(t, shared)\n\tstate, err := readCommitGraphState(shared)\n\tif err != nil || !state.filtersUsed {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a graph in an alternate is not seen: %+v %v\", state, err)\n\t}\n}\n\n// Review F5: a repository whose history cannot be read is a warning, not a\n// silent pass; a root that is not a repository gets no check.\nfunc TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory(t *testing.T) {\n\tif _, err := readCommitGraphState(t.TempDir()); !errors.Is(err, errCommitGraphNotApplicable) {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a directory outside Git: %v, want not applicable\", err)\n\t}\n\trepo := doctorFixture(t)\n\toriginal := commitGraphHistoryThreshold\n\tt.Cleanup(func() { commitGraphHistoryThreshold = original })\n\tcommitGraphHistoryThreshold = 1\n\tcommit := func() {\n\t\tgitInRepo(t, repo, \"-c\", \"user.name=fixture\", \"-c\", \"user.email=fixture@example.invalid\", \"-c\", \"commit.gpgsign=false\", \"commit\", \"-q\", \"--allow-empty\", \"-m\", \"history\")\n\t}\n\tcommit()\n\tcommit()\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"git\", \"-C\", repo, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD~1\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tparent := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\tif err := os.Remove(filepath.Join(repo, \".git\", \"objects\", parent[:2], parent[2:])); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the parent commit is not a loose object: %v\", err)\n\t}\n\tif f := doctorCommitGraphFinding(t, repo); f.Level != \"warn\" || !strings.Contains(f.Message, \"could not be read\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"an unreadable history: %+v\", f)\n\t}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff 2bf2adf147bda0b7d8396e025d8f543d08ed2747 c12c45e0eb9c2351b5aac9c53296242c0f1238a4 -- .github/workflows/ci.yml .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go; cat /var/tmp/closeout/ac-dc.out /var/tmp/closeout/sc-dc.out'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'git diff 2bf2adf147bda0b7d8396e025d8f543d08ed2747 c12c45e0eb9c2351b5aac9c53296242c0f1238a4 -- .github/workflows/ci.yml .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go; cat /var/tmp/closeout/ac-dc.out /var/tmp/closeout/sc-dc.out'","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml\nindex 8512af6c..cdebba85 100644\n--- a/.github/workflows/ci.yml\n+++ b/.github/workflows/ci.yml\n@@ -18,6 +18,12 @@ jobs:\n       # runner where it matters (spec pose-release-boundary-rehearsal).\n       - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n         with: { fetch-depth: 0 }\n+      # Governance reads ask Git for the last commit that touched each\n+      # artifact; on the full history each lookup walks it. The commit-graph\n+      # with changed-path filters answers from the filters (47 ms -> 2 ms a\n+      # lookup; spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph).\n+      - name: Write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters\n+        run: git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths\n       - uses: actions/setup-go@b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e # v7.0.0\n         with: { go-version: \"1.26.10\", cache: true, cache-dependency-path: pose-mcp/go.mod }\n       - name: Go tests (engine drift guard included)\n@@ -117,6 +123,12 @@ jobs:\n       # exactly this reason, reporting a governance defect that did not exist.\n       - uses: actions/checkout@3d3c42e5aac5ba805825da76410c181273ba90b1 # v7.0.1\n         with: { fetch-depth: 0 }\n+      # Governance reads ask Git for the last commit that touched each\n+      # artifact; on the full history each lookup walks it. The commit-graph\n+      # with changed-path filters answers from the filters (47 ms -> 2 ms a\n+      # lookup; spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph).\n+      - name: Write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters\n+        run: git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths\n       - uses: actions/setup-go@b7ad1dad31e06c5925ef5d2fc7ad053ef454303e # v7.0.0\n         with: { go-version: \"1.26.10\", cache: true, cache-dependency-path: pose-mcp/go.mod }\n       - name: Build development pose binary\ndiff --git a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\nindex 966572c1..bb92d120 100644\n--- a/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n+++ b/.pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n@@ -2419,10 +2419,17 @@\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n         },\n+        {\n+          \"name\": \"doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph-integration\",\n+          \"program\": \"go\",\n+          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/cli\", \"-run\", \"TestDoctorRecommendsTheCommitGraph|TestDoctorCommitGraphReportsAnUnreadableHistory\", \"-count=1\"],\n+          \"severity\": \"required\",\n+          \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n+        },\n         {\n           \"name\": \"review-ledger-is-read-once-per-operation-integration\",\n           \"program\": \"go\",\n-          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/pose\", \"-run\", \"TestReviewLedgerIsReadOncePerOperation|TestReviewLedgerWithoutAnOperationIsReadEveryTime|TestReviewLedgerMemoAnswersAsAFreshRead|TestReviewLedgerMemoComputesOnceForConcurrentMisses|TestReviewLedgerMemoGivesIsolatedCopies|TestCopyReviewRunsCoversEveryUnexportedField\", \"-count=1\"],\n+          \"args\": [\"test\", \"./internal/pose\", \"-run\", \"TestReviewLedgerIsReadOncePerOperation|TestReviewLedgerWithoutAnOperationIsReadEveryTime|TestReviewLedgerMemoAnswersAsAFreshRead|TestReviewLedgerMemoComputesOnceForConcurrentMisses|TestReviewLedgerMemoGivesIsolatedCopies|TestCopyReviewRunsCoversEveryUnexportedField|TestReviewLedgerMemoIsNotServedAfterTheOperationEnds\", \"-count=1\"],\n           \"severity\": \"required\",\n           \"evidenceClass\": \"integration\"\n         },\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go\nindex d091206a..86cbac00 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go\n@@ -6,6 +6,7 @@ package cli\n // --yes to actually apply — never silently.\n \n import (\n+\t\"errors\"\n \t\"bytes\"\n \t\"context\"\n \t\"encoding/json\"\n@@ -89,6 +90,10 @@ var doctorFixRegistry = map[string]doctorFix{\n \t\t\treturn err\n \t\t},\n \t},\n+\t\"git.commit-graph\": {\n+\t\tdescribe: \"write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters (git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths)\",\n+\t\tapply:    writeCommitGraph,\n+\t},\n \t\"skills.symlinks\": {\n \t\tdescribe: \"recreate .claude/skills symlinks\",\n \t\tapply: func(root string) error {\n@@ -355,6 +360,23 @@ func runDoctorDiagnostics(locale cliLocale) (root string, findings []doctorFindi\n \t\tadd(\"hooks.pre-commit\", \"ok\", text(\"pre-commit hook installed\", \"pre-commit instalado\"), \"\")\n \t}\n \n+\t// 7b. Git commit-graph. Governance reads ask Git for the last commit that\n+\t// touched each artifact; on a long history each lookup walks it, and a\n+\t// commit-graph with changed-path filters answers it from the filters\n+\t// (spec pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph).\n+\tgraph, graphErr := readCommitGraphState(root)\n+\tswitch {\n+\tcase errors.Is(graphErr, errCommitGraphNotApplicable):\n+\tcase graphErr != nil:\n+\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"warn\", text(\"Git commit-graph state could not be read: \"+graphErr.Error(), \"estado do commit-graph do Git não pôde ser lido: \"+graphErr.Error()), text(\"check that the repository and its object directory are readable\", \"confira se o repositório e o diretório de objetos podem ser lidos\"))\n+\tcase graph.commits < commitGraphHistoryThreshold:\n+\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"ok\", text(fmt.Sprintf(\"history of %d commits does not need a commit-graph\", graph.commits), fmt.Sprintf(\"histórico de %d commits não precisa de commit-graph\", graph.commits)), \"\")\n+\tcase graph.filtersUsed:\n+\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"ok\", text(\"Git answers last-commit lookups from changed-path filters\", \"o Git responde a busca do último commit pelos filtros de caminhos alterados\"), \"\")\n+\tdefault:\n+\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"next\", text(fmt.Sprintf(\"history of %d commits is walked whole by each last-commit lookup: Git has no commit-graph with changed-path filters\", graph.commits), fmt.Sprintf(\"histórico de %d commits percorrido inteiro a cada busca do último commit: o Git não tem commit-graph com filtros de caminhos alterados\", graph.commits)), text(\"run 'git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths' (or 'pose doctor --fix --yes')\", \"rode 'git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths' (ou 'pose doctor --fix --yes')\"))\n+\t}\n+\n \t// 8. Delivery-integrity / artifact-contract policy roots (issue #17): a\n \t// v1.2.0 `pose install`/`pose update --force` could seed\n \t// .pose/policy/{delivery,artifacts}.json with roots copied verbatim from\nartifact.spec=pose-doctor-recommends-the-commit-graph\nartifact.change_set=cs-44a19acf2fe0\nartifact.change_set.base=2bf2adf147bda0b7d8396e025d8f543d08ed2747\nartifact.change_set.head=c12c45e0eb9c2351b5aac9c53296242c0f1238a4\nartifact.change_set.commits=11\nartifact.diff_digest=sha256:62899eecbff266844be4f9dcd1271df3e8656f15fed946f6cd4b1d58a8042045\nartifact.claims=5\nartifact.observed=22\nartifact.findings=973\n[!] warning orphan pose-mcp/internal/cli/update_reports_delivered_state_test.go\n    governed tracked path has no spec provenance claim\n    fix: backfill an explicit claim or add an exact reviewed exclusion\n[!] warning orphan pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_routing_test.go\n    governed tracked path has no spec provenance claim\n    fix: backfill an explicit claim or add an exact reviewed exclusion\n[!] warning orphan pose-mcp/internal/cli/state_hooks_test.go\n    governed tracked path has no spec provenance claim\n    fix: b