{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md; rg -n 'func RawHistoryGit|func.*rawHistory|GIT_TRACE2|git.commit-graph' pose-mcp/internal/pose/git* pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go; rg -n 'commit.graph|commit-graph|doctor' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md'\"","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:93:\t\"git.commit-graph\": {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:94:\t\tdescribe: \"write the Git commit-graph with changed-path filters (git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths)\",\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:371:\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"warn\", text(\"Git commit-graph state could not be read: \"+graphErr.Error(), \"estado do commit-graph do Git não pôde ser lido: \"+graphErr.Error()), text(\"check that the repository and its object directory are readable\", \"confira se o repositório e o diretório de objetos podem ser lidos\"))\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:373:\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"ok\", text(fmt.Sprintf(\"history of %d commits does not need a commit-graph\", graph.commits), fmt.Sprintf(\"histórico de %d commits não precisa de commit-graph\", graph.commits)), \"\")\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:375:\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"ok\", text(\"Git answers last-commit lookups from changed-path filters\", \"o Git responde a busca do último commit pelos filtros de caminhos alterados\"), \"\")\npose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go:377:\t\tadd(\"git.commit-graph\", \"next\", text(fmt.Sprintf(\"history of %d commits is walked whole by each last-commit lookup: Git has no commit-graph with changed-path filters\", graph.commits), fmt.Sprintf(\"histórico de %d commits percorrido inteiro a cada busca do último commit: o Git não tem commit-graph com filtros de caminhos alterados\", graph.commits)), text(\"run 'git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths' (or 'pose doctor --fix --yes')\", \"rode 'git commit-graph write --reachable --changed-paths' (ou 'pose doctor --fix --yes')\"))\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:53:`pose-doctor-guided-remediation` — findings de `pose doctor` ganharam\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:55:blocked), versionado aditivamente via `doctor_schema_version` (JSON prévio\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:56:intacto); `pose doctor --fix` prevê reparos confinados e reversíveis\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:60:`2026-07-19-doctor-guided-remediation-confined-fix-registry.md`. (2)\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:211:  Windows zip, placeholders removidos, E2E clean-host com doctor+check\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:282:  `.pose/specs/pose-doctor-guided-remediation/`, `.pose/specs/pose-brownfield-reference-kits/`,\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:289:  `.pose/adr/2026-07-19-doctor-guided-remediation-confined-fix-registry.md`,\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:67:- `pose doctor` gained two detectable (not auto-fixable — the correct roots\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:74:  has to hand-edit the JSON, but doctor now tells them why and where.\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:87:`doctor_remediation_test.go` (contaminated-roots detection and a\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:104:- The `pose doctor` heuristic (all configured roots missing → warn) is\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:132:  `pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor.go`, `pose-mcp/internal/cli/doctor_remediation_test.go`,\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-15-decision-log-github-issue-closing-keyword-format.md:81:Same owner. If a `pose-doctor`-style check or a spec-frontmatter\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:12:  commands: [\"pose check --strict\", \"pose knowledge-check --strict\", \"pose specs --json\", \"pose followups --json\", \"pose usage --json\", \"pose doctor --json\", \"pose review-plan spec:<slug> --json\", \"pose artifact-check --spec <slug> --json\", \"pose check --json\", \"pose lint-spec <slug> --json\", \"pose history-check --json\", \"pose knowledge-check --json\", \"pose skills-check --json\", \"pose recurrence-check --json\", \"pose index --json\", \"pose state --json\"]\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:102:a boolean that prints JSON to stdout (`specs`, `followups`, `usage`, `doctor`,\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:187:gate, `update`, `doctor`, `index` on large trees):\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:296:- **panels/summary blocks** for a release cut and for `doctor`;\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:377:   `validate`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `release`, `doctor`)?\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
